{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-07_16-54-34-swiftpaxos_epaxos-real-run/agent-source && sed -n '328,335p' replica/replica.go && cd ../draft && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfb={\\\"ref_ids\\\":[\\\"S22-replica-listener\\\",\\\"S3-run-dispatch\\\",\\\"S13-handleCommit\\\",\\\"S31-master-startup\\\"],\n \\\"answered\\\":\\\"An attempt to observe the crash findings inside the replica's own event loop (rather than by calling the handler) did not reach the panic, and the reason is itself a behavioural detail worth recording. The loop spends about ten seconds in peer-latency measurement before it serves anything: ComputeClosestPeers performs twenty rounds of pings and sleeps half a second per round, so a started replica does not enter its select loop, and therefore does not accept client connections, for roughly that long. The harness waited only a couple of seconds and its queued commit was still unprocessed, which is why the run reported that the loop survived; the construction also showed that a listen failure is silent under a non-verbose logger, since the logger's Fatal prints and exits only when verbose. The process-death step of the crash findings therefore remains an inference from the run loop having no recover, while the ten-second measurement phase is now an observed startup property rather than an assumption.\\\",\n \\\"remaining\\\":[\\\"observing the crash inside the loop would need a harness that waits out the measurement phase and provides a listener the sandbox permits, or a package-level injection point the implementation does not have\\\",\n   \\\"the two capacity-limited items (a review for the pending client-reply artifact, unit-plus-review capacity for the reader observation)\\\",\n   \\\"the two paused candidates, the configuration premises and the attribution issue on the superseded C1 artifact\\\"],\n \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Recording the failed construction and the startup-phase observation prevents a future attempt from repeating the same short-wait mistake, and it keeps the crash findings' one remaining inference boundary visible instead of claiming it was observed.\\\",\n \\\"understanding\\\":\\\"updated\\\",\\\"question_updates\\\":{}}\nsub={\\\"action\\\":\\\"research\\\",\\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Records why the run-loop observation did not reach the panic and what it did reveal: a roughly ten-second peer-measurement phase before the loop serves anything, and a silent listen failure under a non-verbose logger. No map object changes and no requirement, premise or earlier observation is affected.\\\",\n \\\"feedback\\\":fb}\njson.dump(sub,open('submission-loop-note.json','w'),indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)\nprint('ok')\nPY\ncd .. && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-07_16-54-34-swiftpaxos_epaxos-real-run --submission submission-loop-note.json 2>&1 | head -6\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
